# Summary
Introduces a metadata growth/DoS risk by persisting completed multipart upload tombstones without any expiry or cleanup outside bucket deletion.
The commit adds a completed_multipart_uploads table and writes a tombstone for every completed multipart upload. AbortMultipartUpload now returns success for completed uploads without deleting the tombstone, and the only cleanup path is during bucket deletion. This means a client with upload permissions can repeatedly complete multipart uploads (optionally deleting the resulting objects) and cause unbounded growth of the metadata DB, leading to potential disk exhaustion or performance degradation.

# Reviewer note
This report was valid.

It was fixed in commit `03639e6` (`Bound completed multipart tombstone retention`), which adds bounded per-bucket retention for completed multipart tombstones and prunes older rows after multipart completion while preserving AWS-compatible `AbortMultipartUpload` idempotence for recent completed uploads.

